    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "s = \"aab\"  ->  [[\"a\", \"a\", \"b\"], [\"aa\", \"b\"]]\ns = \"a\"    ->  [[\"a\"]]\ns = \"aba\"  ->  [[\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"], [\"aba\"]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: choose where the next piece ends"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Each call has a `start` position — everything before it is already split. The choices are where the **next piece ends**: any `end` from `start` to the last index. A choice is allowed only if `s[start..end]` is a palindrome; then recurse from `end + 1`. When `start` reaches the end of the string, the pieces so far are a complete answer. The palindrome check is the pruning — a non-palindrome piece cuts off its whole subtree."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Backtracking with a palindrome check"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "palindrome-partition-tree",
      "caption": "Try each possible next piece; only palindromic pieces are explored further."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function partition(s) {\n  const result = [];\n  const path = [];\n  const isPal = (i, j) => {\n    while (i < j) if (s[i++] !== s[j--]) return false;\n    return true;\n  };\n  const cut = (start) => {\n    if (start === s.length) {                // the whole string is split\n      result.push([...path]);\n      return;\n    }\n    for (let end = start; end < s.length; end++) {\n      if (!isPal(start, end)) continue;      // prune: this piece isn't a palindrome\n      path.push(s.slice(start, end + 1));    // choose the piece s[start..end]\n      cut(end + 1);                          // split the rest\n      path.pop();\n    }\n  };\n  cut(0);\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def partition(s):\n    result, path = [], []\n\n    def cut(start):\n        if start == len(s):               # the whole string is split\n            result.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for end in range(start, len(s)):\n            piece = s[start:end + 1]\n            if piece != piece[::-1]:\n                continue                  # prune: this piece isn't a palindrome\n            path.append(piece)            # choose the piece s[start..end]\n            cut(end + 1)                  # split the rest\n            path.pop()\n\n    cut(0)\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<String>> partition(String s) {\n    List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    cut(s, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid cut(String s, int start, List<String> path, List<List<String>> result) {\n    if (start == s.length()) {                   // the whole string is split\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {\n        if (!isPal(s, start, end)) continue;     // prune: this piece isn't a palindrome\n        path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));   // choose the piece s[start..end]\n        cut(s, end + 1, path, result);           // split the rest\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nboolean isPal(String s, int i, int j) {\n    while (i < j) if (s.charAt(i++) != s.charAt(j--)) return false;\n    return true;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool isPal(const string& s, int i, int j) {\n    while (i < j) if (s[i++] != s[j--]) return false;\n    return true;\n}\n\nvoid cut(const string& s, int start, vector<string>& path, vector<vector<string>>& result) {\n    if (start == (int)s.size()) {                // the whole string is split\n        result.push_back(path);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int end = start; end < (int)s.size(); end++) {\n        if (!isPal(s, start, end)) continue;     // prune: this piece isn't a palindrome\n        path.push_back(s.substr(start, end - start + 1));   // choose the piece s[start..end]\n        cut(s, end + 1, path, result);           // split the rest\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<string>> partition(string s) {\n    vector<vector<string>> result;\n    vector<string> path;\n    cut(s, 0, path, result);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · 2ⁿ)**: a string of n equal letters has 2ⁿ⁻¹ splits, and each palindrome check is O(n). **Space O(n)** for the path and the call stack."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Precompute which pieces are palindromes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The same `s[i..j]` is checked again and again from different branches. Fill a table first: `pal[i][j]` is true when `s[i] == s[j]` and the inside, `pal[i+1][j−1]`, is a palindrome (or there is no inside). Then every check during the search is O(1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function partition(s) {\n  const n = s.length;\n  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(n).fill(false));\n  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {\n      pal[i][j] = s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || pal[i + 1][j - 1]);   // ends match, inside is a palindrome\n    }\n  }\n  const result = [];\n  const path = [];\n  const cut = (start) => {\n    if (start === n) {\n      result.push([...path]);\n      return;\n    }\n    for (let end = start; end < n; end++) {\n      if (!pal[start][end]) continue;        // O(1) check now\n      path.push(s.slice(start, end + 1));\n      cut(end + 1);\n      path.pop();\n    }\n  };\n  cut(0);\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def partition(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    pal = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            pal[i][j] = s[i] == s[j] and (j - i < 2 or pal[i + 1][j - 1])   # ends match, inside too\n    result, path = [], []\n\n    def cut(start):\n        if start == n:\n            result.append(path[:])\n            return\n        for end in range(start, n):\n            if pal[start][end]:           # O(1) check now\n                path.append(s[start:end + 1])\n                cut(end + 1)\n                path.pop()\n\n    cut(0)\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<String>> partition(String s) {\n    int n = s.length();\n    boolean[][] pal = new boolean[n][n];\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n            pal[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || pal[i + 1][j - 1]);   // ends match, inside too\n        }\n    }\n    List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    cut(s, pal, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid cut(String s, boolean[][] pal, int start, List<String> path, List<List<String>> result) {\n    if (start == s.length()) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {\n        if (!pal[start][end]) continue;          // O(1) check now\n        path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));\n        cut(s, pal, end + 1, path, result);\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void cut(const string& s, vector<vector<bool>>& pal, int start, vector<string>& path, vector<vector<string>>& result) {\n    if (start == (int)s.size()) {\n        result.push_back(path);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int end = start; end < (int)s.size(); end++) {\n        if (!pal[start][end]) continue;          // O(1) check now\n        path.push_back(s.substr(start, end - start + 1));\n        cut(s, pal, end + 1, path, result);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<string>> partition(string s) {\n    int n = s.size();\n    vector<vector<bool>> pal(n, vector<bool>(n, false));\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n        for (int j = i; j < n; j++)\n            pal[i][j] = s[i] == s[j] && (j - i < 2 || pal[i + 1][j - 1]);   // ends match, inside too\n    vector<vector<string>> result;\n    vector<string> path;\n    cut(s, pal, 0, path, result);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · 2ⁿ)** still (the output can be that big), but each check is O(1) after an **O(n²)** table. **Space O(n²)** for the table. Backtracking plus a DP table is a common pairing in hard problems."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Backtracking with a palindrome check",
          "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "Up to **2ⁿ⁻¹** ways to split, and each piece is checked in **O(n)**.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The path and the call stack."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Precompute which pieces are palindromes",
          "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "The output can still be that big, but each check is **O(1)** after an O(n²) table.",
          "space": "O(n²)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **n × n** palindrome table."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**One letter** — `[[\"a\"]]`.",
        "**All the same letter** — every split works: 2ⁿ⁻¹ answers.",
        "**No long palindromes** — `\"abc\"`: only single letters.",
        "**Copy the path** when recording an answer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Palindrome Partitioning II** — only the **fewest** cuts: dynamic programming, no listing needed.",
        "**Word Break II** — split into dictionary words instead of palindromes: the same backtracking.",
        "**Restore IP Addresses** — split into four pieces, each a number from 0 to 255."
      ]
    }
